  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a line-following robot see the line?",
      "a": "It uses infrared sensors that shine light at the floor and measure the reflection. A light floor reflects a lot and a dark line reflects little, so the robot can tell them apart.",
      "asks": [
        "What sensors does a line follower use?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a line follower decide which way to turn?",
      "a": "If the left sensor sees the line, it turns left by slowing the left wheel; if the right sensor sees it, it turns right. The robot keeps correcting towards the line.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a line follower do when it drifts?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a robot with no steering wheel turn?",
      "a": "It spins its left and right wheels at different speeds. The faster wheel travels further, swinging the robot round towards the slower side. This is differential drive.",
      "asks": [
        "What is differential drive?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do line followers wobble?",
      "a": "Simple on-off steering overcorrects one way, then the other. Using more sensors and PID control lets the robot steer gently and follow smoothly.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you make a line follower smoother?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are line-following robots used?",
      "a": "Automated guided carts in factories, warehouses and hospitals follow lines, magnetic tape or floor markers to carry parts and supplies along fixed routes.",
      "asks": [
        "Are line-following robots used in real life?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Most line-following robots use infrared reflectance sensors that tell dark surfaces from light ones.",
    "Automated guided vehicles have carried parts in factories since the 1950s."
  ],
